Early spring evening in mid-March, on an old wooden table beside a window. A thick hand-bound field guide notebook lies open, stitched with twine, its cream pages filled with tiny illegible handwriting and small hand-drawn sketches of round little animals, little huts and garden plots, with paper index tabs in three soft colors sticking out of the page edges. A small smooth grey river pebble rests on the corner of the open page as a paperweight. Beside the notebook, a twig of budding branches in a glass of water, a loose ball of twine and a small pair of scissors. Warm low golden sunlight slants across the table, long soft shadows, outside the window bare branches with the first tiny buds. The feeling of a small question that quietly grew into a whole book. Wide composition, no person.
